13:24:44 | INFO | 🤖 Target initial answer: # Why White Loads Show Dye Transfer More Than Colored Loads Actually, dye transfer *does* happen in colored loads too—it's just harder to notice! Here's what's really going on: ## The Physics is the Same When you wash a red item, some dye molecules always leach into the water, especially if: - The item is new or hasn't been washed many times before - The water is hot - The item wasn't colorfast-treated well during manufacturing ## Why You Notice It on White White is essentially a blank canvas. Even a tiny amount of red dye deposited on white fabric creates an obvious, high-contrast pink tint. Your eye is extremely sensitive to seeing color appear where there was none before. ## Why You Don't Notice It on Colors In a load of mixed colors: 1. **Camouflage effect** – A little red dye landing on a blue shirt might shift it slightly toward purple, but it's subtle and easy to miss 2. **Dark colors hide it well** – Small amounts of transferred dye barely show up on already-dark or patterned fabric 3. **Similar base tones** – If the load has warm tones already (tans, oranges, other reds), added red dye blends in rather than standing out ## The Real Lesson The dye transfer is happening at a similar rate regardless of what's in the load—white just acts like a "canary in the coal mine" that reveals a problem that's usually invisible. This is exactly why laundry advice says to wash new, dark, or brightly colored items separately for the first several washes: they're still shedding excess dye, you just can't always see it happening.
